  <p>
      Most JWT explainers make you read the theory first and try an example later, if at all.
      Do it the other way around here: open the <a href="/tools/jwt-decoder">JWT Decoder</a> in
      another tab, paste in any token you have lying around (or one from <a href="https://jwt.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">jwt.io</a>'s example), and
      follow along as we take it apart.
    </p>

    <h2>What a JWT actually is</h2>
    <p>
      A JSON Web Token (JWT) is three Base64URL-encoded segments joined by dots:
      <code>header.payload.signature</code>. The header names the signing algorithm, the payload
      carries claims — a user ID, an expiry timestamp, whatever the issuer decided to include — and
      the signature proves the first two segments haven't been tampered with since they were signed.
    </p>

    <p>Here's an actual token, dots included:</p>
    <pre class="!my-3 !py-3"><code>eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJzdWIiOiIxMjM0NTY3ODkwIiwibmFtZSI6IkpvaG4gRG9lIiwiaWF0IjoxNTE2MjM5MDIyfQ.SflKxwRJSMeKKF2QT4fwpMeJf36POk6yJV_adQssw5c</code></pre>
    <p>Split at the dots and decoded, those three segments are:</p>
    <pre class="!my-3 !py-3"><code>{`// Header — algorithm + token type
{"alg": "HS256", "typ": "JWT"}

// Payload — the actual claims
{"sub": "1234567890", "name": "John Doe", "iat": 1516239022}

// Signature — HMACSHA256(base64UrlEncode(header) + "." + base64UrlEncode(payload), secret)
// not human-readable — this is what a verifier recomputes and compares`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Paste that same token into the <a href="/tools/jwt-decoder">JWT Decoder</a> and you'll get
      exactly this header and payload back — no secret required, which is the whole point of the
      next section.
    </p>
    <p>
      The part that trips up almost everyone new to this: <strong>the header and payload are encoded,
      not encrypted</strong>. Anyone holding the token — a browser extension, a proxy, a coworker with
      access to your logs — can decode and read every claim inside it without knowing any secret. Only
      the signature is cryptographically protected, and all it proves is authenticity, not
      confidentiality. Paste a token into the decoder above and you'll see this immediately: no key
      required, the payload just appears.
    </p>

    <h2>The standard claims worth knowing</h2>
    <p>
      RFC 7519 reserves a handful of short claim names so different systems agree on what they mean —
      none are mandatory unless your library enforces them, but you'll see all six constantly:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>iss</code> (issuer) — who created the token, typically your auth service's URL.</li>
      <li><code>sub</code> (subject) — who the token is about, usually a user ID.</li>
      <li><code>aud</code> (audience) — who the token is meant for; a resource server should reject a token issued for a different one.</li>
      <li><code>exp</code> (expiration) — a Unix timestamp after which the token must be rejected outright.</li>
      <li><code>iat</code> (issued at) — when the token was created, useful for auditing and computing age.</li>
      <li><code>nbf</code> (not before) — a Unix timestamp before which the token isn't valid yet, useful for pre-issued tokens.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Anything else is a private claim — your own field names for whatever the application needs, like <code>role</code> or <code>plan</code>.</p>

    <h2>Why that distinction changes what you put in a token</h2>
    <p>
      Because the payload is readable by anyone, it's the wrong place for a password, a full credit
      card number, or anything else that needs to stay secret. Put identifiers and permissions in
      there — <code>sub</code>, <code>role</code>, <code>exp</code> — not secrets. Paste any token you're
      unsure about into the <a href="/tools/jwt-structure-explainer">JWT Structure Explainer</a> for a
      visual, side-by-side breakdown of exactly which part is which.
    </p>

    <h2>HS256 vs RS256: the algorithm choice that decides who can issue tokens</h2>
    <p>
      The header's <code>alg</code> field isn't a formality — it decides whether one service or many
      can safely issue tokens. <code>HS256</code> (HMAC-SHA256) is symmetric: the same secret signs and
      verifies, so every service that needs to check a token also needs that exact secret — and could
      therefore also forge one. <code>RS256</code> (RSA) and <code>ES256</code> (ECDSA) are asymmetric:
      one private key signs, and a public key — safe to hand out freely — verifies. A single auth
      service can keep the private key to itself while a dozen downstream APIs verify tokens with only
      the public key, never gaining the ability to mint new ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use <code>HS256</code> when one service issues and checks its own tokens. Reach for
      <code>RS256</code> the moment a second service needs to verify tokens it didn't issue — the
      alternative is handing that service your signing secret, which quietly turns "can verify" into
      "can impersonate anyone."
    </p>

    <h2>Decode, verify, and validate aren't the same step</h2>
    <p>
      These three words get used interchangeably, and mixing them up explains a lot of confusing bug
      reports. <strong>Decoding</strong> just reverses the Base64URL encoding to read the JSON — no
      cryptography involved, no proof the token is real. <strong>Verifying</strong> recomputes the
      signature from the header and payload using the secret (or public key) and confirms it matches —
      the step that actually proves the token wasn't forged or altered. <strong>Validating</strong> goes
      further still: beyond a matching signature, it checks that required claims are present and sane —
      not expired, not used before its <code>nbf</code> time, issued for the right <code>aud</code>. A
      library's <code>verify()</code> call usually does all three at once, but the distinction matters
      the day you reach for a lower-level function that only does one of them.
    </p>

    <h2>The mistake that causes the most real bugs: not checking expiry</h2>
    <p>
      A JWT with an <code>exp</code> claim in the past is still a perfectly valid, correctly-signed
      token — validity and expiry are two separate checks, and skipping the second one is a common
      cause of "randomly logged out" or "works sometimes" auth bugs. Before chasing a more exotic
      explanation for an intermittent 401, paste the token into the <a href="/tools/jwt-expiry-checker">JWT Expiry Checker</a> and rule out the simplest cause
      first — it usually is the simplest cause.
    </p>

    <h2>The mistake that causes the most real security incidents: a weak signing secret</h2>
    <p>
      A JWT's entire security model rests on the signing secret staying unguessable. A short,
      memorable secret — or worse, a placeholder like <code>"secret"</code> or
      <code>"changeme"</code> left over from a tutorial — can be brute-forced, letting an attacker
      forge valid tokens for any user, completely bypassing authentication. This isn't theoretical;
      leaked or guessable JWT secrets are a recurring finding in real security audits. Run your
      actual signing secret through the <a href="/tools/jwt-secret-checker">JWT Secret Strength Checker</a> before it ever reaches production, and generate a proper one with the <a href="/tools/token-generator">Secure Token Generator</a> if it doesn't hold up.
    </p>

    <h2>The attack that isn't about guessing your secret: alg:none</h2>
    <p>
      Not every JWT attack targets a weak secret — some target careless verification code instead. The
      <code>alg</code> field in the header names the algorithm to use, and if a server's JWT library
      trusts that field instead of pinning to an expected algorithm, an attacker can rewrite the header
      to <code>{`{"alg": "none"}`}</code>, strip the signature entirely, and submit a token with any
      payload they like. A verifier that naively checks "does this token have a valid signature for
      whatever algorithm it claims to use" sees "none," requires no signature, and accepts it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A related variant targets asymmetric setups: an attacker takes a server's public RS256 key —
      often not secret at all — and resubmits the token signed with HS256, using that public key as the
      HMAC secret. A verifier that trusts the token's own <code>alg</code> field will use the same
      public key to "verify" an HMAC signature the attacker could compute themselves, since the key was
      never secret to begin with. Both attacks share one root cause: letting the token decide its own
      verification algorithm. Every mainstream JWT library lets you pin the expected algorithm(s)
      explicitly — use it, and never let the token's header make that decision for you.
    </p>

    <h2>Where should the token actually live: localStorage or a cookie?</h2>
    <p>
      This gets argued about more than almost anything else in JWT auth, and the honest answer is that
      both options trade one risk for another. <code>localStorage</code> is simple and readable only by
      your own JavaScript — until an XSS vulnerability lets an attacker's script read it too, at which
      point the token is fully compromised with no recourse until it expires. An <code>httpOnly</code>
      cookie can't be read by JavaScript at all, closing off that specific theft path, but cookies bring
      their own concern, CSRF, which needs its own mitigation — a <code>SameSite</code> attribute, or a
      separate anti-CSRF token.
    </p>
    <p>
      There's no option that's safe by default, only a choice of which attack class you're defending
      against. If your app already has to be rigorous about XSS anyway — most do — an
      <code>httpOnly</code>, <code>Secure</code>, <code>SameSite=Strict</code> cookie is generally the
      safer default for browser-based apps. For a token consumed by a non-browser client (a mobile app,
      a server-to-server call), this whole question doesn't apply — store it in whatever secure storage
      that platform already provides.
    </p>

    <h2>If a token is stolen, the attacker doesn't need your password</h2>
    <p>
      A stolen JWT is a stolen identity for as long as it stays valid — the entire point of the token is
      that possessing it, not proving anything further, is what gets you access. There's no username or
      password check on each request, so a stolen token that hasn't expired works exactly as well for an
      attacker as it does for the real user, from any device, until it expires or you've built a way to
      revoke it early. This is exactly why short expiry times matter more for JWTs than they intuitively
      seem to: expiry isn't about tidiness, it's your only real recovery window once a token has leaked.
    </p>

    <h2>JWT vs sessions: there's no universally "correct" choice</h2>
    <p>
      JWTs push state onto the client, which makes them easy to verify across any number of stateless
      servers — no shared session store required. Sessions keep state on the server, which makes
      instant revocation trivial (delete the session row) in a way JWTs fundamentally aren't, since a
      signed token stays valid until it expires no matter what happens server-side. Neither is
      "better" outright; the right choice depends on whether you need effortless horizontal scaling
      or effortless revocation more.
    </p>

    <h2>Common JWT errors and what actually causes them</h2>
    <p>Most JWT errors map to one of a small handful of causes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>"invalid signature"</strong> — the secret or key used to verify doesn't match the one used to sign, or the header/payload was modified after signing, including by a proxy that re-serializes the request.</li>
      <li><strong>"jwt expired"</strong> — usually self-explanatory, but check whether the client and server clocks actually agree before assuming the token itself is at fault; clock drift causes more of these than people expect.</li>
      <li><strong>"jwt malformed"</strong> — the string isn't three Base64URL segments separated by dots, often from double-encoding the token, trimming it, or forwarding only part of it.</li>
      <li><strong>"missing authorization header" / "invalid bearer token"</strong> — the client isn't sending <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> at all, or is sending it with the wrong prefix or a stray quote character left over from copy-pasting.</li>
      <li><strong>"jwt not active"</strong> — the token's <code>nbf</code> claim is set later than the verifying server's current clock time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Paste the failing token into the <a href="/tools/jwt-decoder">JWT Decoder</a> first — most of these
      are diagnosable just from the decoded header and payload, before you touch any server code.
    </p>

    <h2>A minimal checklist before you ship JWT auth</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Signing secret is long, random, and generated — not typed by hand.</li>
      <li>Every request handler verifies the signature server-side; nothing trusts a decoded payload on its own.</li>
      <li>The verifier pins the expected algorithm explicitly — it never lets the token's own <code>alg</code> header decide how it gets checked.</li>
      <li><code>exp</code> is set, and your code actually checks it — decoding alone won't reject an expired token for you.</li>
      <li>No passwords, full card numbers, or other secrets sit in the payload, encoded or not.</li>
      <li>You have a plan for revocation (short expiry plus refresh tokens, or a blocklist) if a token needs to die before it naturally expires.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      That covers the part that actually causes production incidents. For the full breakdown of all
      three token segments side by side, see the <a href="/tools/jwt-structure-explainer">JWT Structure Explainer</a> — or just
      keep pasting tokens into the <a href="/tools/jwt-decoder">decoder</a> until the structure feels
      obvious, which usually doesn't take long.
    </p>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <p><strong>What does it mean to decode a JWT?</strong> Decoding reverses the Base64URL encoding on the header and payload to read the plain JSON underneath — no secret key needed, since encoding isn't encryption. Verifying is the separate, stronger step: it recomputes the signature with the secret and confirms it matches.</p>
    <p><strong>What's the difference between validating and verifying a JWT?</strong> Verifying only confirms the signature matches, proving the token wasn't altered. Validating goes further — it also checks that required claims make sense: the token hasn't expired, isn't being used before its <code>nbf</code> time, and was issued for the right audience.</p>
    <p><strong>What does a JWT authentication example look like end to end?</strong> A user logs in with a password; the server verifies it and signs a JWT containing the user ID and an expiry, returning it to the client. The client sends that token back on every request, usually in an <code>Authorization</code> header, and the server verifies the signature and expiry each time instead of checking a session database.</p>
    <p><strong>Is there a JWT token generator?</strong> JWTs are normally generated server-side by your auth library using your app's real signing secret, not by a standalone browser tool — a client-side generator can't know that secret. The <a href="/tools/token-generator">Secure Token Generator</a> can generate the strong secret your server signs with, and the <a href="/tools/jwt-decoder">JWT Decoder</a> can inspect tokens you already have.</p>
    <p><strong>How do I generate a JWT secret key?</strong> Use a cryptographically secure random generator — never a memorable phrase or a leftover tutorial placeholder. Run it through the <a href="/tools/jwt-secret-checker">JWT Secret Strength Checker</a> to confirm it's not brute-forceable.</p>
    <p><strong>Can I edit a JWT token?</strong> You can decode it, edit the payload JSON, and re-encode it — but that produces an invalid signature unless you re-sign with the original secret. A server correctly verifying signatures rejects the edited token, which is exactly the tamper-evidence JWTs are designed to provide.</p>
    <p><strong>What happens if my JWT is stolen?</strong> Whoever holds it can act as you until it expires or you revoke it — there's no password check on top of a valid token. This is why short expiry times and a revocation plan matter more for JWTs than they might seem to at first.</p>
</BlogPostLayout>
